  "subtitle": "The Egyptian sculpture gallery",
  "blurb": "A coffered stone hall at the end of the day, its walls the colour of sand, lit low and warm between two colonnades of fluted columns standing clear of the wall from floor to ceiling.",
  "corridorNote": "Modelled on the Egyptian sculpture gallery at the British Museum — Room 4: a strictly symmetrical one-point hall under a deeply coffered ceiling of thick square beams, every recess stepping upward in receding treads with no curve anywhere in it. Down each side runs a colonnade of colossal fluted columns standing a full drum clear of the wall, from floor to ceiling, on the bay divisions rather than in front of the hang. The walls behind them are sand-coloured plaster over a heavy plinth course; tall windows high on one side take a low sun across the room; the sculpture has been taken out, so what stands on the centre line is carved marble benches; and the floor is wide courses of matte stone crossing the hall, chalky grey against charcoal.",
